You can inspect the exact bit pattern behind any float — useful for debugging precision issues:

```c
#include <string.h>
float f = 5.0f;
uint32_t bits;
memcpy(&bits, &f, sizeof(bits));
printf("%08X\n", bits);  // 40A00000

// 0x40A00000 = 0100 0000 1010 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000
// sign=0, exponent=10000001 (129-127=2), mantissa=.01 -> 1.25 * 2^2 = 5.0
```

This `memcpy`-based reinterpretation (never a plain cast!) is the standard, well-defined way to view a float's raw bits without invoking undefined behavior.
